Wireshark: Capture Filters vs Display Filters
1. The main difference
Feature
Capture filter
Display filter
When it is applied
Before and during capture.
After capture, on packets already stored.
Syntax
BPF (Berkeley Packet Filter), the same style used by tcpdump.
Wireshark's own field-based syntax, such as tcp.port == 80.
What it does
Packets that do not match are never saved.
Packets that do not match are only hidden. They stay in the file.
Can be changed later?
No. Dropped packets are lost.
Yes. You can change it at any time.
Where to type it
Capture options, before starting.
The green filter bar on top of the packet list.
Best use
Busy networks, to keep file size small, or to capture one host only.
Detailed analysis, hunting and reports.


Colour of the filter bar: green means valid syntax, red means an error, yellow means a warning.
Common mistake: typing display syntax such as ip.addr == 10.0.0.1 into the capture filter box. The capture filter needs host 10.0.0.1.
Tip: capture broadly, then narrow with display filters, unless the network is very busy.
2. Ten working capture filter examples (BPF)
#
Capture filter
Meaning
1
host 192.168.56.104
Traffic to or from this host.
2
src host 192.168.56.106
Traffic sent by this host.
3
dst host 192.168.56.104
Traffic sent to this host.
4
net 192.168.56.0/24
Traffic within this subnet.
5
port 80
Traffic on port 80 (TCP or UDP).
6
tcp port 22
SSH traffic only.
7
udp port 53
DNS traffic over UDP.
8
icmp
Ping and other ICMP messages.
9
not arp
Everything except ARP.
10
host 192.168.56.104 and tcp port 80
HTTP traffic with one server (filters can be combined with and, or, not).


3. Ten working display filter examples
#
Display filter
Meaning
1
ip.addr == 192.168.56.104
Packets to or from this IP.
2
tcp.port == 80
TCP traffic with source or destination port 80.
3
http.request.method == "GET"
HTTP GET requests.
4
dns
All DNS packets.
5
dns.qry.name contains "example"
DNS queries containing this text.
6
icmp.type == 8
ICMP echo requests (pings).
7
arp
All ARP packets.
8
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
First packet of a handshake. A burst of these is typical of a SYN port scan.
9
tcp.flags.reset == 1
Reset packets.
10
frame contains "password"
Any packet containing this text. Useful for spotting clear-text credentials.


4. Operators you can use in display filters
Operator
Meaning
Example
== or !=
Equal or not equal
ip.src == 10.0.0.5
&& or and
Both conditions true
tcp.port == 80 && ip.addr == 10.0.0.5
|| or or
Either condition true
dns || arp
! or not
Negation
!(arp || dns)
contains
Field or packet contains text
http.host contains "test"


Remember: capture filters decide what is recorded. Display filters decide what you see. For the Week 2 lab, capture the Kali-to-Ubuntu traffic and use tcp.flags.syn==1 && tcp.flags.ack==0 to show the Nmap SYN scan burst.


